History of the Hindu-Arabic Numeral System (with Focus on Ashoka and Brahmi Origins)

The claim in the original post—that Ashoka’s edicts from the 3rd century BCE already display the Hindu-Arabic numeral system—is only partly accurate. The shapes of many modern digits do descend directly from the Brahmi numerals used under the Mauryan Empire, but the complete modern system (positional decimal notation + zero) developed centuries later.

1. Brahmi Numerals in the Ashoka Period (c. 250–232 BCE)
The earliest securely dated numerals in India appear in the rock and pillar edicts of Emperor Ashoka. These are known as Brahmi numerals. They are the direct graphic ancestors of the modern Hindu-Arabic digits 1–9.

Key features of the early system:
Separate symbols existed for 1–9, for the tens (10, 20, 30…90), and for higher powers (100, 1000).
It was additive and multiplicative, not positional. For example, 256 might be written using a special symbol for 200 + a symbol for 50 + a symbol for 6.
There was no zero.
Early forms of 1, 2, and 3 were simple strokes (vertical or horizontal). 4 often resembled a cross or “+”.

2. Intermediate Development (1st–6th centuries CE)
The numerals continued to evolve regionally:
Gupta numerals** (4th–6th centuries) became more cursive and began to look closer to modern shapes.
Different regional styles (Kushan, Kshatrapa, Valabhi, etc.) produced variations that later branched into modern Indian scripts (Devanagari, Bengali, Tamil, etc.) as well as the forms that traveled westward.

The system remained non-positional for a long time. Large numbers were still expressed by combining distinct symbols for different powers of ten.

3. Birth of the Positional System and Zero (c. 5th–9th centuries CE)
The decisive breakthrough occurred in India:
A true place-value (positional) decimal system began to appear around the 5th–7th centuries CE. Instead of special symbols for tens and hundreds, the position of a digit determined its value.
Zero* (Sanskrit *śūnya, “void” or “empty”) was introduced both as a placeholder and as a number in its own right. Early forms were a simple dot; later a small circle.
Mathematician Brahmagupta (c. 628 CE) gave the first clear rules for arithmetic operations involving zero in his Brahmasphutasiddhanta.
The earliest known dated inscriptions using zero as a circle in a positional system appear in the late 7th–9th centuries (e.g., Gwalior temple inscription of 876 CE showing “270”). Earlier manuscript evidence (Bakhshali manuscript) may push the placeholder use of a dot back further.

By the end of the 9th century the modern-style system (nine digits + zero, fully positional) had largely replaced the older additive Brahmi system across most of India.

4. Transmission Westward
The system reached the Islamic world by the late 8th–early 9th century.
Persian mathematician Al-Khwarizmi wrote On the Calculation with Hindu Numerals (c. 825 CE), which popularized the system among Arabic-speaking scholars. The word “algorithm” derives from his name.
Arabic mathematicians called them “Hindu numerals.” Two main branches developed: Eastern Arabic (still used in parts of the Middle East) and Western Arabic (Ghubar/“dust” numerals), which later became the European forms.

5. Arrival in Europe and Modern Standardization
The system entered Europe mainly through Spain and Sicily in the 10th–12th centuries.
Fibonacci* (Leonardo of Pisa) strongly promoted it in his *Liber Abaci (1202), demonstrating its superiority over Roman numerals for calculation.
The shapes continued to evolve; by the 15th–16th centuries they closely resembled the digits used today in the West.

The chart below illustrates the later stages of this graphical evolution:

Summary Timeline
| Period              | Key Development                                      |
|---------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| 3rd century BCE     | Brahmi numerals in Ashoka’s edicts (additive, no zero) |
| 1st–6th centuries CE| Regional evolution (Gupta forms, etc.)               |
| 5th–7th centuries CE| Positional notation + zero emerges                   |
| 9th century CE      | Fully developed system in India; transmitted to Arabs |
| 12th–15th centuries | Adopted and standardized in Europe                   |

In short, Ashoka’s Mauryan inscriptions give us the ancestral shapes of the digits we still use. The revolutionary system—place value plus zero—that made those shapes so powerful was perfected in India roughly a thousand years later and then carried westward by Arab scholars.
